  {
    "episode_id": 49330,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49330.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "Absolutely. When we were designing the first CARES Act package, some of the first steps we took were very bipartisan. They were designed to provide resources for our hospitals and our health clinics and our next tranche of legislation dealt with the need to do testing and tracing things that we still don't have in sufficient numbers and speed right now. But the first major economic package was the CARES Act. And as we were working on the CARES Act, it became very apparent to me hearing from a lot of your members and others that there were millions and millions of people who are going to be left out of unemployment compensation because they don't traditionally get it. if they're part of the gig economy, if they're freelancers, if they work contract to contract, if they don't have the traditional W-2 employment. And it was vitally important that we make sure that they not be left behind. So I helped to organize an effort in the house to include people who were independent contractors and gig workers and freelancers. And we did. So for the first time in this enhanced unemployment benefit is $600 a week. We covered people who have that kind of work. But what we have found subsequent to that is that there are still people being left out. And this is a subset of people who have mixed income. So they may have some W2 income, but traditionally got most of their income on a 1099. So they may have, for example, been getting some small amount of royalties from year to year on a W2, but most of their income has come from periodic jobs. from, you know, as you say, getting that phone call. And as a result of having a modest amount of W-2 income, but losing all of their 1099 income, they were getting left out. They were either getting very little unemployment compensation or not at all. And so when we discovered this group of people with mixed income were really being hurt by this, we looked to see, is this something the states can remedy on their own? Do we need to write new law? And I think it's the conclusion that we're going to have to write new law to fix this. So I introduced the bill this week to do that to try to address those with mixed income. I'm hoping to move this bill either as a standalone bill or probably even better if we can make it a part of this package that we're now negotiating between Democrats and Republicans. And so, yes, we were able to help many millions, maybe tens of millions of people who are good workers, but there were still a great many who were left out who have mixed income and we're trying to fix that now.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
