  {
    "episode_id": 49330,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49330.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "You know, I think that there's certainly some recognition of this. You know, I think most of the members of Congress understand that businesses and workers in different industries that require a venue where people congregate, where the first to lose their work and will be the last to regain it. There's a general recognition of that. I mean, everybody can go by, for example, their local movie house and see that it's closed and it's been closed a long time. but there's obviously unique issues affecting the music industry. There are unique venues in my district like the Troubadour, but in other places too in Tennessee and New York and elsewhere, where the impact is much greater because of the concentration of that kind of work and employment and creativity. And for that reason, we need to reach out to these other representatives from the country Their constituents may not be as directly impacted, but if the music goes away, their constituents are gonna be deeply impacted. And what they also need to understand, and this is something we struggle even in the best of times with, is members of Congress think about the music industry, like when they think about the film or TV industry, they think about the stars.",
