  {
    "episode_id": 49330,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49330.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 29,
    "turn_text": "Well, we took up in the House the Justice and Placing Act, as you mentioned, probably the most far-reaching police reform Congress has ever taken up. It would do away with the kind of qualified immunity that has protected so many police officers that have committed egregious violence. It was racial profiling. It bans chokeholds and no-knock warrants and drug cases. It requires reform in police departments. It incentivizes oversight and reform. And we need to continue to fight to get that passed at the moment it is in the Senate graveyard, Mitch McConnell's graveyard where so many other bills go to die, but we're not going to give up until that becomes the law of the land. It's important to recognize though that racism within policing is only one very small subset of a much larger problem. Racism within policing is only part of a problem of racism within the criminal justice system more broadly. which includes racism and sentencing problems with cross racial IDs and a whole host of other issues. And of course, the criminal justice system is just one institution. I'm working on a bill right now that I hope to introduce fairly soon to deal with the problem of racism and bias in the delivery of healthcare. It's bad enough that we're seeing in this pandemic such terrible disparate outcomes for black Americans, for Latino Americans and others, it's bad enough when you can't get access to good care. But it's also makes the problem that much worse than even when you get access. If the care you get is unequal, inequitable because of the color of your skin. And that happens too. And I have a bill that is designed to address that problem of inequity in the performance of healthcare within the system. It's my hope, Paul, that one of the results of this pandemic is that, as you say, it gives us a chance to confront major problems facing the country and build the country back better.",
