    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49330.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "It's a nice little bright spot in the midst of this to see the Supreme Court come back with the decisions they have, for example, with the LGBTQ issues and where before that decision, you know, you could get married, you know, if you were gay, but you could lose your job the next day and there was no recourse. I was very pleased to see that decision come down from the Supreme Court. I'm gonna jump to something that is key for all music creators right now. Songwriters are heavily regulated. Our income is, we're the most heavily regulated small business owners that are probably in this country and all, you know. And when it comes to small business men, I'm perfect, put on a frazz cap. I'm a small business man, you know, so but we're heavily regulated. Our income is more regulated than Google, Amazon, Apple, Spotify, Facebook, Netflix, radio, and television. We're songwriters. We're not really a threat to the nation, but we are deeply, deeply, deeply regulated. And part of the reason is a consent decree, which we operate under, which is an agreement with the Department of Justice, which was created in 1941, 1941. The internet was not exactly, I mean, music was very different. It's all changed. It's all changed, and yet we continue to be constricted by this consent degree. We're trying to modernize the consent degree to the level of the playing field when we negotiate with tech and media giants. I mean, they have the advantage they love, and they love the status quo, because they can kind of keep their thumb on the scale with that. We just want to make it better. We want to make people, you know, what's important, what it's ultimately about is food on the table and gas in the car, you know, for the hard-working songwriters who are providing the music for this country. Do you agree that the deodoration considered the imbalance as they review the consent degrees and try to give songwriters a fair chance?",
