  {
    "episode_id": 49330,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49330.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 33,
    "turn_text": "Absolutely. Look, FDR was a great president, but there are a few things that have changed since 1941. the consent decree ought to change with them. I don't know that you can point to any other industry that has changed more in terms of the modality of how that industry performs in the music industry. I mean, it has changed with great next speed. Even within the last 10 years, there have been dramatic change. I do think we need to revisit the consent decree and revise it, and I know what our priority it is for songwriters and composers. It's part of a, you know, frankly, a broader problem and challenge, and that is you have a concentration of great power and wealth, sometimes in the hands of a few companies, but even more as a general matter, which has led to a historic and now yawning gap between rich and poor in this country with a middle class that is struggling more than ever to survive. And part of addressing that is giving individuals more collective bargaining power of these massive companies with their concentration of wealth. I think the attacks on organized labor, for example, of the last several decades are part of the reason why we have such huge disparities in wealth now. But you can also see it in the music industry, where how can an individual songwriter ever hope to negotiate from the position of strength with a behemoth like Google or Spotify or Pandora? I mean, you just can't. And so I think it's important to work within organizations like ASCAP. But also it's important that the Congress and the administration, the Justice Department, look at the mechanism with which songwriters and composers are marginalized economically. The fact that things have changed so dramatically in the decades since we structured compensation in the music industry and figure out how do we bring this up to the modern age? How do we equalize the bargaining position so that we can continue to enjoy all this creative talent? I don't know about you, Paul. I've probably never listened to more music in my life than right now.",
