    "turn_text": "Well, you know, I've certainly seen, although I haven't had a chance to watch one of the on-screen concerts yet, and I'm long overdue. But I have seen new stories about how people are performing now, and they're doing it remotely. I was very proud to see the LA choir, for example, singing together in a remarkable performance where they all sang separately. But nonetheless, it was through the miracle of technology. turned into a beautiful choral music. But I think in terms of, you know, happy to share with you my own music tastes, which are frankly extraordinarily diverse. They range from the classical to kings of Leon to Matthews, Billy Joel, Steely Dan, And I was in a Pearl Jam phase for a long time. And, but you know, I just give you one illustration of a song that I played recently, which I hadn't listened to for years. But it's to me a perfect illustration of what music can do. It's a Herb Alpert song called This Guys in Love with You. It was our first dance at my wedding, but even more significant in a way is it was one of my mother's favorite songs, which was one of the reasons why I wanted to make it our first dance song in my wedding. My mother passed away several years ago, but I can't listen to that song without thinking of her and without seeing an image of her walking around the house, humming that to herself or singing that to herself. And that's the thing about music. It can so vividly bring you back to times of your life and moments. It's so evocative of things that are going on. I can't imagine seeing films without music. And then, by the way, while on the subject of films and music, I do have a recommendation for your members. And I may have been the last person to see this film. So if I am, forgive me. But searching for Sugarman",
