  {
    "episode_id": 49330,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49330.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 45,
    "turn_text": "You know, it is so important. And, you know, I've often made the observation that what's really holding back our country is our broken form of governance right now. And the kind of bipartisanship that you're talking about, the collaboration you're talking about, it does still go on. It is more rare, tragically much more rare. You know, to give you an illustration for 18 years and up until he left the Congress two years ago, a very conservative Texan named John Culberson. And I worked together on NASA every year. He was the senior most Republican on the subcommittee that funded NASA. I was the senior most Democrat. We were inseparable. We both have a love for science and space science and I love, for example, the work that the Jet Propulsion Laboratory in my district does, and he loved the work that, you know, that manned space flight and other efforts in his Texas area did. And we were, we could not be parted even by NASA, much to NASA's chagrin sometimes. Now, we didn't talk about issues where we had strong disagreements. We disagreed about taxes and immigration and just about everything else. We looked for the common ground and worked together. Those kind of partnerships still occur, but they're all too rare. I'll be very candid with you, though, Paul. The tone is really set from the top. As long as we have a president who is set on dividing us, we are going to be divided, not just as a Congress, but as a country. And so I'm not particularly optimistic about the rebirth of bipartisanship with Donald Trump as president because that is simply not where he's coming from. And I don't think it will ever be where he's coming from.",
