    "turn_text": "Well, you know, the kind of the hierarchy of effective ways to advocate The very best is something that's just not possible right now, which is meeting together with your representatives, local, state, federal. What we're doing right now is probably the next best thing, which is a video conference. I've been doing a lot of video conferences with my constituents. I've been doing video coffees, video town halls, telephone town halls. The personal interaction really matters. It's one thing when you read about or you hear abstractly, oh, this is a really hard time for performing artists, it's a hard time for songwriters, it's a hard time for composers. They were the first to lose their jobs and they'll be the last to regain them. It's another when you talk to someone directly who says, I lost my job, I lost it back in February. And there's just no sign of when I'm gonna get it back. And I don't get much unemployment because I mixed income or here's my circumstance. seeing someone hearing their story. It really makes a difference. And so that kind of personal connection is really important. It's important to work through organizations as well, because you don't want to have to necessarily reinvent the wheel. And the organizations can direct your focus. And if the priority, for example, there are real lifeline, economic lifeline issues right now for songwriters and composers but there are also longer-term issues like the consent decree. And working through organizations can help prioritize, okay, this is really what we need to focus on right now. So in addition to the kind of in-person Zoom kind of meetings, emails, calls to offices, our offices are still open even if they're not people all the time, make your voice heard.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
