    "turn_idx": 52,
    "turn_text": "I told a story that night about how I was on the light rail in Pasadena and there was a father singing to his young daughter who she looked to be about nine And every time he tried singing something, she says, I don't like that one. And he would try different songs. And she said, I didn't like that one either. And I didn't know these people. But I, you know, I felt as a member of Congress, I could interrupt their conversation. So I did. And I said, well, let me try this. And I started singing, eight, no sunshine when she's gone. And that little girl looked up at me with those big eyes. And she said, yeah, I don't like that one either. But I hope it wasn't his song, it must have been the way I sang it.",
