  {
    "episode_id": 49330,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49330.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 61,
    "turn_text": "I'm not going to run. This is the greatest job that I've ever had. This is the most adult thing I've ever done in my life as well. It requires a certain discipline. And it's just an honor. It's a great honor. I was elected in 2009. And one of my greatest memories is shortly after I was elected. I went to the White House for the Gershwin Prize. Actually, it was Helen and how David and Birkbeck were being given the Gershwin Prize. and met for the first time, you know, President Obama and the First Lady. And I had just been elected President of ASCAP. He had just been elected President of the United States. And I said, this may sound outrageous, but would you call me Mr. President, Mr. President? He said, Mr. President, it would be an honor. And it's one of those little phrases you take and you hang up in your office and go, that's something I will always remember and all. I have to tell you, I always remember this conversation as well. We've never really had a chance to have a real conversation like this, and it's a real honor. I will tell you that there is a certain, people mistake kindness for weakness sometimes, but it's a foolish thing to do. You have an elegance of kindness and a caring, and the work that you're doing on a daily basis, not only for us as music creators, but what you're doing for all of us as citizens of the United States is not something that I'll ever have enough time or language skills to properly thank you. It's what you've done has been remarkable, and we are forever grateful.",
